  "seoDescription": "Convert the data to float64 and return the array formed by selecting elements along that axis using integer array indexing.",
  "solved": false,
  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Select elements along a specified axis using integer array fancy indexing.\n\nUnlike basic slicing which returns memory views, fancy indexing always produces an independent copy of the data.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nY = \\operatorname{take}(X, \\text{indices}, \\text{axis})\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Convert data to float64 array\n2. Apply integer index array along target axis\n3. Return selected subarray\n\n### Returns\nExtracted NumPy array formatted as nested list.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Fancy Indexing\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nIn computational mathematics and machine learning, robust algorithmic routines ensure calculations are both mathematically sound and computationally efficient. **Fancy Indexing** implements a core computational building block.\n\nUnlike basic slicing which returns memory views, fancy indexing always produces an independent copy of the data. In this problem, the goal is to: select elements along a specified axis using integer array fancy indexing.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nY = \\operatorname{take}(X, \\text{indices}, \\text{axis})\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Convert data to float64 array\n2. **Phase 2**: Apply integer index array along target axis\n3. **Phase 3**: Return selected subarray\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Algorithmic Complexity**: Designing routines with optimal time and space complexity ensures they scale gracefully to large datasets.\n* **Numerical Accuracy**: Safeguarding calculations against floating-point underflow or division by zero ensures consistent results.\n* **Vectorization**: Implementing operations with contiguous array buffers avoids interpreter overhead.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: data = [[10.0, 20.0, 30.0], [40.0, 50.0, 60.0], [70.0, 80.0, 90.0]], indices = [0, 2], axis = 0\n\nOutput: [[10.0, 20.0, 30.0], [70.0, 80.0, 90.0]]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated numpy_fancy_indexing under standard test conditions."
  ],
  "requirements": "* Function must be named `numpy_fancy_indexing`\n* Return value must match the exact expected structure and types\n* Handle edge cases cleanly and maintain numerical stability\n* Must run efficiently without redundant memory allocations",
  "hints": [
    "Verify the dimensions of inputs before computing transformations in `numpy_fancy_indexing`.",
    "Utilize vectorized operations rather than manual loops for optimal performance."
